diagnosis_text:
The reported symptom — periodic latency spikes and route flapping every minute or two on the pc0↔pc12 path — is not confirmed by any live evidence in the topology.

Phase 1 reachability showed 0% packet loss in both directions (pc0→pc12 at 1.3 ms RTT, pc12→pc0 at 1.5 ms RTT). The entire Phase 4 broad search was completed: infra_sweep and l2_snapshot (Phase A) returned zero flags; ospf_snapshot (Phase B) flagged three devices, all of which were investigated and explained; tc_snapshot was clean; pressure_sweep and dhcp_link_history (Phase D) returned zero suspects.

The three ospf_snapshot flags were examined individually: r12 and r13 have no OSPF configuration because they are EIGRP routers (EIGRP AS 100), which is by design — r11 is the OSPF-EIGRP boundary that redistributes between protocols. r8 has a static area 0.0.0.2 misconfiguration (its eth1/BB segment is in an orphaned area with no peer, and BB has no other attached devices), but r8's OSPF adjacencies to r4 (via VLINK0, 9m25s uptime) and r5 (9m45s uptime) are both Full and stable, and r8 is not on the pc0↔pc12 forwarding path.

FRR daemon logs on r1 and r11 confirm the backbone has been completely quiescent since the lab started (~19:42); there are no adjacency drops, SPF recalculations, or route changes recorded after the initial convergence. No crash-restart loop, kill script, cron job, or stress process was found on any device. Without any mechanism or historical trace, the intermittent instability claim is unconfirmed.
=== END ===